NAME
    asm - the YACC1 assembler (RC/asm's dialect, on the machine)

SYNOPSIS
    asm SRC [OUT]
    asm -h SRC [OUT]

DESCRIPTION
    Assembles SRC in two passes and writes OUT. A SRC without a '.'
    gets .ASM (asm HELLO reads HELLO.ASM). The source dialect is the
    host assembler's (software/assembler, RC/asm with yacc1.def): what
    y1cc writes, what the ROM monitor and BASIC are written in, and
    the output is byte for byte what the host makes of the source.

    Without -h, OUT (default: SRC without its extension) is a program
    file: the bytes from the first address written to the last, gaps
    filled with zeros, with load = that first address and exec = the
    address of END (else the load address). `run OUT` or typing its
    name runs it. The code must go up in address (an ORG back is
    refused: use -h).

    With -h, OUT (default: SRC's name + .IMG) is Intel hex, the same
    text as the host's `asm NAME -d=yacc1` with -h: 16-byte records, a
    new record at every ORG and DS, the end record :00000001ff.

    The summary goes to stdout: "OUT: N bytes, N labels, load AAAA
    exec AAAA". An error goes to the screen with its line number and
    the line, and assembling goes on to find the rest; after any error
    OUT is deleted (an older file of that name too).

SOURCE
    [label:] mnemonic [operands] [; comment], one per line. Every line
    is upper-cased outside single quotes, so labels are case-folded
    and "text" is upper case ('text' keeps its case). Numbers: 123,
    0FFH, 'A'. Expressions: + - * / & ! (or), (x).0 the low byte,
    (x).1 the high byte, $ the address, parentheses. A label used
    before its definition counts as 1 in pass 1, so an ORG, DS or EQU
    operand should only use labels defined above it. Directives: ORG,
    END, EQU (a value or a register name), DB (numbers, strings),
    DB HIGH, DW (big-endian words), DS n, INCLUDE file (two deep).
    RC/asm's quirks are kept: a leading minus is dropped (LDAI -1 is
    01; write 0-1), and '.' binds first (write (x+4).0, not x+4.0).

OPTIONS
    -h    write Intel hex instead of a program file

LIMITS
    16,639 bytes of labels, 5 + the name's length each (about 1,360
    of y1cc's labels; the biggest compiler pass, cc8, has 1,326),
    29 characters a label, 254 a line, 45 tokens an expression.
    Files up to 64K (Y1/OS keeps 16-bit positions). Not supported
    (errors here, accepted by RC/asm): MACRO/ENDM, PUBLIC, EXTERN, LIB,
    '/' on values beyond 16 bits, an EQU value outside -65535..65535,
    a label or code past $FFFF. OUT is written through Y1/OS's one
    write handle: asm cannot run inside a > redirect or a pipe (it
    says "asm: cannot create OUT").

EXAMPLES
    asm HELLO.ASM             HELLO, a program: `run HELLO` runs it
    asm -h MONITOR.ASM M.IMG  the ROM monitor as Intel hex
    asm /S/CAT.ASM /T/MYCAT   a program into another directory

NOTES
    Written for Y1/OS 2026-09-25 (P8X had a native assembler for its
    own ISA; this one is new). The instruction table is generated from
    software/assembler/yacc1.def (tools/gen_y1_optab.py), so it cannot
    drift from the host assembler; tests/asm compares the two on every
    source in the tree. Speed: 1,499 lines of y1cc output (27K, cat)
    in 13.4M instructions.

